! The Ingest Canon ~ one path, born-annotated, single-write

The golden path for getting //any// chat session + its telemetry into the palace, locked by research
(field + code) on 2026-06-27 under the operator's full manaoio. The un-pono shape it retires:
''write-bare-then-enrich-in-a-second-pass'' (ELT load-then-transform — a race window, an
"eventually annotated" drawer). The cure: ''annotate at the source, write once.''

<<~ scale name=`flow` ranks=`source -> producer(exchange-assembler) -> capture-verb -> nalu(born-annotate+WAL) -> flush(mine --source ndjson) -> mempalace-drawer`>>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-nine-locks`>>

!! The nine locks (decisions, not options)

<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`1-born-annotated` params=`every record enters as {content · source_file · lar_*-metadata} computed at enqueue, content+annotation in ONE flush — zero un-annotated window (schema-on-write)`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`1b-grammar-in-vm` params=```the annotation is computed by the daemon-plane TW5 VM's GRADIENT MODULE — a tw5 js module that runs the NATIVE TW5 parser flow (`$tw.wiki.parseText` with our `<<~` overlay wikirule loaded) to build the FULL TW5+memetic-wikitext parse tree for each nalu item, then GRADES the sigil nodes GRACEFULLY: the grammar is CREATIVE not deterministic (it drops, partial-renders, drifts mid-turn), so the gradient reads how cleanly each sigil manifests (provisional→canon; a complete sigil grades high, a drifted/partial one low). ONE grammar, ONE runtime — the daemon-plane engine IS the parser (we DON'T fork Jeremy's parser, we overlay it). RETIRE turn-harvest.ts / the mesh harvestTurnGradient (the VM-free regex shadow). [lar-telemetry canon]```>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`2-kappa` params=`ONE ingest path. live AND historical through the SAME producer→capture→nalu; history = REPLAY, never a second code path. Retire the convo_miner+writeback fork as primary (Kreps/Kappa)`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`3-exchange-grain` params=`the DRAWER = the exchange pair (Q+A) — the self-contained recall unit. The producer assembles pairs → one CaptureRecord per pair. (per-message gradient bearing-index stays a SEPARATE artifact, not a drawer)`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`4-writeback-demoted` params=`writeback NEVER runs per-ingest — ONLY on a deriver-version bump (lar_hv + HARVEST_VERSION in lockstep), as a one-shot version-gated backfill across wings. its sole legitimate role: re-derive lar_* from stored content without re-ingesting`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`5-writeback-lock` params=`the writeback IS a writer → it MUST take the SAME mine_palace_lock the mine holds (chroma's get→update is no single SQLite transaction; SQLITE_BUSY guards a statement, not a read-modify-write → lost-update risk). on busy → retry. THE one real code change beyond producer unification`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`6-transport` params=`transport is a FLUSH detail under the one path: daemon-SHORE when concurrent (live), DIRECT fresh-process + busy-retry when sole bulk writer (isolated backfill). one isomorphic CaptureFlush verb, two transports`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`7-idempotency` params=`content-key watermark (sha of exchange text, NOT uuid) = re-ingest is a no-op + survives across surfaces; mempalace source_file dedup backs it. version-gate (lar_hv) governs re-derivation, ORTHOGONAL`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`8-busy-is-retryable` params=`the palace lock is the cross-process coordination — a held lock (MineAlreadyRunning) is a BUSY signal (= SQLITE_BUSY), not an error; every writer retries with backoff+jitter. live+bulk coexist through the one lock (verified: 0 failures, pave 33619)`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`9-one-runtime` params=`ONE RUNTIME — the daemon-plane TW5 VM is the SOLE ingest + gradient runtime. DROP every outside-VM path: the VM-free regex reader stands nowhere (no regex shadow, no VM-free projection, no fallback — #/the-harvester-burn), no CLI-side annotate, no daemon-down direct-mine. Verbatim-always rides the DURABLE TRANSCRIPT (harness-written, always on disk) REPLAYED through the VM when it wakes — never an outside-VM mine. One grammar, one runtime, no fork to keep in lockstep`>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`10-superset` params=```memetic-wikitext ⊃ TW5 wikitext — THREE NESTED TIERS: host TW5 wikitext (Jeremy's grammar, untouched) ⊂ +OVERLAY (our `<<~` SharktoothSigils, tokenizer-claimed FIRST) ⊂ +GUEST grammars (nakama, `hana`-bounded blocks). TWO realizations: (R) RENDER — the lares plugin registers `<<~` as a wikirule in TW5's own $tw wikiparser so TW5 parses ALL native wikitext, our overlay claims only sigil tokens (the Markdoc/MDX tokenizer-overlay pattern, host untouched). GRADIENT-RENDER SYMMETRY: a chat-born sigil typed into a LIVE wikitext input renders on the SAME gradient as the telemetry parse — complete=rich, drifted/partial=graded best-effort, NEVER a hard parse-error. The gradient lives in the WIKIRULE → one overlay, both surfaces (chat ingest · live wiki render); (A) meme-ast PARSE-TREE — standalone, opaque to TW5: only sigils are typed nodes, host wikitext rides as verbatim TextNodes (byte-exact raw slice). The host-passthrough is BY DESIGN, not a gap — host wikitext round-trips byte-exact via TextNode+verbatim; TW5-typed nodes deferred (no consumer needs structural queries over host wikitext). Guest grammar plugs in via an interpreter tiddler at lar:///…/hana-interpreter/<key>; the bounded hana block fences it from host+overlay tokens```>>
<<~ has entity=`Lock` name=`11-drawer-schema` params=```DROP the flat lar_* index. DRAWER = { verbatim content (the document/embedding) + metadata{ `lar_hv`:int · `lar_ast`:JSON-string of the GRADED ''full TW5+memetic-wikitext parse tree'' } }. The tree is built by the daemon-plane's NATIVE TW5 parser flow (`$tw.wiki.parseText` with our `<<~` overlay wikirule loaded) — so HOST wikitext is TYPED (links/macros/headings/transclusions are real TW5 nodes) AND our sigils are typed, in ONE tree that RE-HYDRATES NATIVELY in any VM island (it IS the engine's own parse tree). NOT the standalone host-opaque meme-ast (that serves the disk-projection edges). The AST holds the whole gradient (the flat fields only PROJECTED from it — redundant and lossy). KEEP FLAT only: `lar_hv` (the idempotency watermark, read every sweep client-side — never re-parse the tree for a version#) + the existing non-lar `wing` (the one chroma `where` filter). chroma metadata is flat (str|num|bool) so the AST rides as a JSON STRING (unfilterable by `where` — and nothing filters lar_* today, blast radius ~0). EXTRINSIC provenance (lar_surface · lar_agent_handle · lar_parent_handle · lar_root_handle · lar_sidechain · lar_ffz — NOT parse-derivable from prose) folds into the AST as an `envelope` provenance node, so the tree stays the single structural source. READ: semantic vector recall on verbatim (+ optional where{wing}) → re-hydrate `lar_ast` in the VM island → structural predicates (band≥X, voice∈{}, drift, aim) POST-recall — strictly MORE capable than today's flat filter```>>

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/migration`>>

!! Migration (the circle-back work, after FFZ)

''BUILD (ordered):'' (1) ''register the turn sigils'' as gradient-valued SharktoothSigil tids —
`lares aim/yield · confidence · hud · ward · oracle · syad` (word + gradient-reading + render) so the
engine parses TURNS, not just documents. (2) ''the VM gradient module'' (lararium-tw5): run the NATIVE
`$tw.wiki.parseText` flow (TW5's parser + our `<<~` overlay wikirule) → the FULL TW5+memetic parse
tree (host wikitext typed + sigils typed) → walk + GRADE the sigil nodes (complete=high, drifted/
unclosed=low; the grade-on-a-gradient) → the graded tree + an `envelope` provenance node (surface/
handles/sidechain/ffz). Serializable + re-hydrates natively as the engine's own parse tree. (3) ''wire the cap→VM shore'' — thread `ctx.tw5` to the annotate; the cap calls the VM
module (annotate is sync, `parseMemeText` sync in-worker). (4) ''drawer-write'' — the annotate →
`lar_ast = JSON.stringify(graded AST + envelope)` + `lar_hv`; DROP the flat `lar_*`; rewrite
`LAR_SCHEMA` (adapter.py) to declare only `lar_hv`+`lar_ast`; bump `LAR_HV` 6→7 (lockstep
telemetry-writeback.ts:32 + drawer_io.py:32) → next harvest re-projects stale drawers' METADATA
(verbatim untouched — a metadata re-harvest, not a re-ingest). (5) ''read-side helper'' (lararium-tw5):
re-hydrate `lar_ast` + structural-filter POST-recall. (6) ''exchange-assembler'' — the capture leg rides
PYTHON (`capture_sources.py`, whose `_turn_key` reads the turn key); route historical through the VM,
Kappa replay. (7) `mine_palace_lock`+busy-retry around
the version-gated `writebackWing`/`drawer_io cmd_apply` re-derive ceremony.

''RETIRE (one runtime — no fallback):'' the VM-free regex shadow ENTIRELY (burned whole —
#/the-harvester-burn) · the FLAT `lar_*` projection (band/voices/aim/… → all in the AST now) · `convo_miner
--extract exchange` (pairing moves into the producer; the VM is the sole decomposer) · the daemon-down
direct-mine fallback (verbatim-always = the durable transcript replayed through the VM, not an
outside-VM mine) · `writebackWing`-as-per-ingest (→ version-bump backfill only). No outside-VM path,
no flat-index duplication survives. (`CaptureRecord` type · `ndjson.py` adapter · the `wing` filter: unchanged.)

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-meme`>>

!! The canon, in eight lines

> ONE PATH (Kappa): every chat+telemetry source ingests through the capture-nalu — live and
> historical alike; history is REPLAY through the same producer, never a second code path.
> BORN-ANNOTATED single-write: the record enters mempalace as {content, source_file, lar_*} computed
> at enqueue, content and annotation in ONE flush — zero un-annotated window. DRAWER GRAIN = the
> exchange pair (Q+A), the self-contained recall unit. IDEMPOTENT by content-hash watermark;
> RE-DERIVED only on a lar_hv/HARVEST_VERSION bump (the writeback's sole role — schema-evolution
> backfill, never per-ingest ELT). The writeback is a writer: it takes the SAME mine_palace_lock the
> mine holds. Transport is a flush detail: daemon-shore when concurrent, direct+busy-retry when sole.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/gradient-build`>>

!! The gradient build ~ examined gaps + grade-on-a-gradient (2026-06-27)

The grammar is self-hosted: a tiddler tagged `lar:///ha.ka.ba/tags/SharktoothSigil` teaches the
parser a word (grammar-cache.ts reads `lar-*` fields → SigilRule; 97 sigil tiddlers today). The
AST (meme-ast) parses the DOCUMENT sigils (ahu · pranala · edge-sugar · control · guest-grammar …).

''THE TURN SIGILS STAND REGISTERED.'' `lares` · `set` · `stance` · `mu` · `aperture` ·
`ooda-ha` · `drift-ward` · `oracle` · `persona` · `carry` — each carries a tiddler, and each renders
its own definition rather than falling to water. The turn frame the boot seed declares stands on
the shelf beside the document sigils, so the VM reads a TURN the way it reads a document, and no
regex shadow reads the frame beside it.

''AND THE READER OUTLIVES THE GRAMMAR THAT WROTE IT.'' `confidence` · `hud` · `ward` keep their
tiddlers as PATTERN-ONLY entries: the tree recognises them and the page shows them verbatim. A corpus
states ONE grammar; a reader tolerates every grammar it will meet, and a transcript written before
the panel gathered its gauges still decomposes.

''GRADE ON A GRADIENT — the builder is binary where it should grade'' (the creative-grammar cure):
* `meme-ast/builder.ts:277-281` DROPS unclosed/malformed frames → instead emit a ''MalformedSigil /''
''  PartialNode'' with a parse-confidence (`<<~ ahu ( <4` = opener+intent, broken close → ~0.4;
  `<<~`-far-from-close → ~0.2; no opener → prose 0). "Likely-a-sigil-but-drifted," not silent water.
* per-sigil ''form-confidence'': full head-plus-params = high · bare head = mid ·
  numeric-fail = low. The node is GRADIENT-VALUED, never pass/fail (the harvester's law, in the VM).
* degraded VOICE ladder (full+mask > full > bare-known > looks-like-voice) · edge-strictness · ahu
  nesting-depth — all binary today, all gradient candidates.

''SECONDARY GAPS:'' the `\sigil` pragma parser is a STUB (lar-sigil-pragma.ts:11-13, "full body parse
= future talk-story") → operator-authored sigil defs don't load back. COVERAGE: the parser knows the
structural sigils, and the turn sigils stand registered (above).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-harvester-burn`>>

!! The harvester burn ~ what the fire took, where each part went

''Lock 9 enacted (operator rulings 2026-10-31, rounds 2 and 3; Train Q row Q6, o-takiage).'' The
outside-VM turn readers burned whole, with everything that fed only on them. ''Old turns get mined
later by a flexible method''; until then no TypeScript or Python reader parses a turn's frame.

''What burned:''
* The mesh capture readers: `turn-harvest`, `bearing-harvest`, the turn half of `build-patch`
  (the `lar_*` patch builder and its hall routing) and the recall `stamp-filter`.
* The tw5 form layer's turn readers: `bearing-ast`, `move-skeleton`, `self-read-harvester`.
* The two VM harvesters, `capture-annotate-vm` and `query-derive-vm`, with the island channel the
  recall derive rode (`daemon:derive-skeleton-request`/`-result`).
* `lares sense pour <transcript>` (the bearing index and the rewind diff it fed), the worldline
  tree's bearing join, and the recall verb's TypeScript stamp post-filter: `--agent`/`--surface`
  ride to the recall coordinator, whose signature holds every filter.
* The Python turn readers: `form_encoder.py` (the move-skeleton encoder and its serve holder),
  `loci_io.py` (the drawer export/apply writer), and the adapter's nine turn-gradient fields.
* `tools/turn-parity.mjs` and its witness.

''Where each surviving part went:''
* The worldline handle derivations (surface · agent · handle · root handle), the fork frontier's
  `BranchContext`, `fnv1a8` and `LAR_HV` moved into `branch-frontier.ts`; `@lararium/mesh/harvest`
  trims to the pure barrel of `ffz-project`, `branch-frontier` and `gone-turns`.
* `FormPalaceStore` moved into `form_store.py`, where the plane caps store and read the FORM plane.
* `pour --all` (the re-pave) and `pour --writeback` keep their doors; the re-pave's baseline keeps
  its state home.

''Three placeholders stand, the one ruled exception to the no-shims law:'' the lar-telemetry
projection (`telemetry-writeback.ts`), the recall form leg (`makeFormSearch`) and the FORM store
surface (`makeFormPalace`). Each keeps its export shape, answers empty, names the ruling in its
source, and carries one pinning test. They retire when the sensorium arc returns, which waits on the
founded-Lararium proof; a road row tracks them.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/integration`>>

!! Full integration ~ TW5-community-grounded (2026-06-27, research-locked)

Plugin, NOT a fork — squarely the welcomed path (the official Markdown plugin is the precedent: an
alternate syntax shipped as a plugin). Five findings, all sourced (talk.tiddlywiki, Jermolene/TW5,
linonetwo's tw-react + TidGi):

# ''Registration is ALREADY canonical.'' `memetic-parser.ts` subclasses WikiParser for content-type
   `text/memetic-wikitext` (deny-list now EMPTY → native rule flow unchanged); wikirules load via the
   stock `module-type: wikirule` path (`createClassesFromModules`). `<<~` claims by DISJOINT MATCH —
   TW5's `macrocallinline` skips it (`~` isn't a legal macro-name start), so no ordering-luck, no fork.
   We sidestep TW5's global token-collision pitfall TWO ways: our own content-type AND the non-colliding
   `<<~` namespace. Keep both. (One live test: confirm no `<<~ WORD …>>` validates as a macro call.)

# ''SERIALIZATION RESOLVED — store the parse TREE as DATA, never the parser/widgets.'' TW5's
   `wiki.parseText()` returns a Parser INSTANCE (holds a live `wiki` ref — NOT serializable); its `.tree`
   is plain nested objects (`{type,tag,attributes,children,text,start,end}`, JSON primitives only) →
   round-trips cleanly. So `lar_ast` = the extracted `.tree` (drop the parser wrapper). Widget refs/
   functions appear only at the later `makeWidget()` stage — never store those.

# ''RE-HYDRATION (the VM-island model).'' Rebuild the widget tree NATIVELY in the island —
   `$tw.wiki.makeTranscludeWidget(title, {document: $tw.fakeDocument, importPageMacros:true})` →
   `node.render(container)` → `node.refresh()` (the canon `core/modules/startup/render.js` path; fakedom
   partial → LinkedOM the node-side drop-in). linonetwo's prior art confirms it: tw-react LIVE-MOUNTS
   (never transpiles; `useWidget` ingests a parse-tree node → live widget), TidGi runs headless TW per
   `worker_threads` workspace with an IPC syncadaptor (= our VM-in-worker islands). Serialize DATA,
   rehydrate WIDGETS natively — never serialize widgets.

# ''GRADIENT-RENDER — the gap is real + small.'' Today the wikirule hard-matches: unrecognized/partial
   → `{type:"text", text:verbatim}` (lar-sigil.ts:132,157, the `__literal__` path) — survives as raw
   text, never graded. TW5 is fail-soft (unmatched→plaintext, unknown-widget→visible "Undefined
   widget", `$error`→`tc-error` span) BUT has NO per-widget try/catch (a JS throw isn't contained). THE
   RULE: the wikirule ALWAYS emits a node, NEVER throws, NEVER falls through (it owns `this.parser.pos`).
   complete → `macrocall` (rich cascade, as today); drifted/partial → a NEW `lar-sigil-graded` widget
   node `{type, attributes:{raw, confidence, reason}}` → a widget reading EVERY attribute defaultably →
   best-effort confidence-graded (`tc-error`-styled) span. Same gradient, both surfaces. (Finish the
   `lar-sigil-pragma.ts` stub too.)

# ''PONO ENGAGEMENT.'' Ship as a plugin (`$:/plugins/<pub>/<name>`) on our own content-type + `<<~`
   namespace; sign the UnaMesa CLA; announce in the talk.tiddlywiki Plugins category; NO `$:/core`
   wikitext edits. Jeremy's ethos welcomes plugin extension. Optional later: `module-type:
   wikiruleserializer` for lossless round-trip.

<<~/ahu>>

